# How to Read Toolkit Outputs

## Read these first

### The Four Takes

These four short sections are written in everyday language. They come near the end of the report, just before the P8 Gate. For most people they are the most useful part of the report.

- **Certainty Take** — which supports still point back, and whether they sit together
- **Uncertainty Take** — what was isolated, what stayed open, and where two supports clash without falling out
- **Reflexive Take** — pressure inside the supports that still held
- **Recap Take** — the shape of this run: what held, what did not, and the yield type

Many people stop here.

### Executive Yield

This block sits near the top. It is a snapshot of the run, not a verdict on the world.

**Yield Type** is the overall shape of this run — Balanced, Tensive, Tempered, or Raw/Regressed. Some runs name two of these together. The label is the runner's judgment; the numbers govern.

**Secondary Coherence Score** measures how coherently the surviving supports point back at your input. It is computed only on the ones that were not isolated. It is a major diagnostic of that mesh. It is not the meaning of the cup, the event, or the claim.

Among the supports that still held, these bands are typical. These ranges are working conventions, not tested against use.

- **Above 0.78** — very strong mesh
- **0.52 – 0.78** — balanced or strong
- **0.25 to just below 0.52** — low-traction
- **N/A** — every listed support was isolated

A high score among survivors does not erase the leftover pile. A lower score does not mean the thing is empty.

**Isolation Residual** is the share of listed supports that did not hold this time (the isolation density). It sits next to the Secondary Coherence Score. The two numbers are two facts. Do not mash them into one score and call that the meaning.

**Tensive**, the number in this block (not the yield type of the same name), is one formal way of holding those two facts together: how strong the surviving mesh is, given how much was refused. It is not a second meaning-number.

The same block also prints the three pieces that made the Secondary Coherence Score — mean alignment, mean inter-coherence, and mean traction — so you can see the arithmetic.

## Then look at the picture

### Secondary Synthesis Ledger and Traction Flow Diagram

The ledger is the cast of this run that still holds. Each row is a support that came forward and still points back, marked Self-pointing or Other-pointing, with its alignment, inter-coherence, and traction. Those are the holders.

Supports that came forward and do not hold this up are not in the ledger. Their traction fell to or below the threshold. They are excluded from the scores above, and they are listed as Synthesis Refusal in the Uncertainty Table.

The Traction Flow Diagram is the same cast as a tree: Self-pointing holders on one branch, Other-pointing holders on the other, flowing into the Secondary Coherence Score and Tensive. Isolation Residual is parked to one side as a density. It is not a third branch. Low-traction holders stay on their branch. They are weak. They are not leftover. You do not have to read every note.

### Uncertainty Table

This is the leftover of this run, plus other kinds of openness that are not leftover.

- **Synthesis Refusal** — listed supports that do not point back. These are the isolated ones, the same items as in the Hard Isolation Summary.
- **Non-relational gaps, generative pauses, and similar rows** — places where the look stayed uncertain or paused. They are not refused, and they are not counted in Isolation Residual.
- **Retained Openness** — what a run leaves open by design, for example a P8 door still open. It stays outside Isolation Residual.

A clash between two holders is not leftover either.

What never came forward is not on this table. Do not hunt it.

## If you want more detail

**Run Metadata** — how many supports were listed, how many were isolated, the isolation density again in short form, the uncertainty after isolation, and input variance.

**Hard Isolation Summary** — a compact restatement of the refusal pile. If nothing was isolated, this section says so.

**Contrast & Non-Relational Gap Summary** and **Audit Summary** — two short paragraphs on what this look was contrasting, and whether the retained mesh hangs together.

**Inferred Relator Mode Distribution** — whether the retained supports are mostly pointing at the input itself or outward at other things. Only those two modes are in the distribution. Isolation Residual is parked outside that pair. It is not a third mode.

**Tempered Certainty Ledger** — the holders again, with a note about where each one is still under pressure.

**Required Claims for Secondary Truth Ledger** — the claims the retained supports needed in order to hold. Secondary Claim Temper is the stability of that list. A shaky row is a weak required claim. It is not leftover.

**Metric Evolution Table** and **Supporting Diagnostics** — a technical view of how the numbers moved. Most readers can skip these. Resonance, Stability, Reflexive Tension, and the rest are supporting instruments. Meaning Tension is a named dark slot with no formula and no figure. It is not meaning.

If Light Output was on, the tables collapse into one narrative. The same facts are still in it: every yield, the Secondary Coherence Score, the key tension, every tempered value, and every claim-stability score.

## A few practical notes

The result is provisional. Every report ends at a P8 gate: the current yield may stand, or you may change the input, change the supports, look again, or stop.

Running the same input more than once can help. Some numbers shift. The overall picture — what held, what did not — tends to stay recognizable. Three runs usually give a picture of the variation and of which supports keep being used.

You pick the input. You can pick the toggles. Two honest runs can disagree. The report is not a judge.

If you want the same look without the numbers, Volume One has a one-page version that uses a pen.

For filled reports, see the [Sample Toolkit Runs](https://github.com/Expressionalism/Expressionalism/blob/main/Expressionalism%20Sample%20Runs.md).

The toolkit does not deliver a final judgment. It shows what held together in this run, and what stayed open. The ordinary thing continues.
